---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GameFrame from '../../components/GameFrame.astro';
import FAQPage from '../../../vendor/site-toolkit/packages/schema/src/FAQPage.astro';

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'What are the rules of Star Battle?',
		answer:
			'Place stars so that every row, every column and every bold-outlined region holds exactly one star. Two stars may never touch, not even diagonally.',
	},
	{
		question: 'What do the dots do?',
		answer:
			'Tap a square once to drop a dot, which is your own note that no star goes there. Tap again for a star. Dots are never checked, so use them freely.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Are there bigger versions with two stars per row?',
		answer:
			'Yes, larger boards often use two stars per row, column and region. The boards here use one star each, which is the friendliest way in.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Does each board have one answer?',
		answer: 'Yes. A solver checks that exactly one solution exists, and a second solver that reasons like a person has to be able to finish the board. Gentle boards need only confined regions. Tricky boards also need a group pattern, where several regions share the same few rows or columns.',
	},
];
---
<Layout
	title="Star Battle puzzle: play free with layered hints"
	description="Play Star Battle free: one star in every row, column and region, and no two stars touching. Four board sizes, undo, keyboard play, and hints that reveal one layer at a time."
>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="game-head">
			<h1>Star Battle</h1>
			<p>One star per row, column and region. Stars never touch.</p>
		</div>
		<GameFrame
			sizes={[6, 7, 8, 9]}
			nudge1="Tap for a place to look"
			nudge2="Tap for how to think about it"
			nudge3="Tap to reveal one star"
		>
			<ul class="legend" slot="legend">
				<li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-star"></use></svg>Star (key S)</li>
				<li><span class="dot" style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:currentColor;opacity:.6;margin:6px"></span>Dot: no star here (key D)</li>
				<li>Tap once for a dot, twice for a star, three times to clear.</li>
				<li>Bold lines are region borders. Each region has a small letter in one corner so hints can name it.</li>
				<li>Striped red squares break a rule right now.</li>
			</ul>
		</GameFrame>
	</div>
	<div class="wrap-narrow prose">
		<h2>How to play Star Battle</h2>
		<p>The board is cut into coloured regions by bold lines. You place stars under three rules.</p>
		<ol>
			<li>Each row has exactly one star.</li>
			<li>Each column has exactly one star.</li>
			<li>Each region has exactly one star.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>On top of that, no two stars can touch, and that includes corners.</p>

		<h2>Where to start</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Small regions first. A region with only two or three squares has very few places for its star. Try them before anything else.</li>
			<li>Each star rules out squares. Once one is down, its whole row and column, its region, and the eight squares around it are off limits. Dot them.</li>
			<li>Trapped regions. If a region sits entirely inside one row, that row's star belongs to the region, so the rest of the row can be dotted out.</li>
			<li>Borrowing. When two regions together occupy only two rows, those two rows spend their stars on them. Nothing else in those rows can hold one.</li>
		</ul>

		<div class="note">
			Hints follow the same steps a person would take. Nudge 1 highlights where to look, nudge 2 says what pattern is there, and nudge 3 tells you what to do with it.
			If one of your stars is wrong, the hints say so before anything else. See <a href="/fair-hints/">how hints work</a>.
		</div>

		<h2>Questions</h2>
		<div class="faq">
			{faq.map((f) => (
				<details>
					<summary>{f.question}</summary>
					<p>{f.answer}</p>
				</details>
			))}
		</div>
	</div>
	<FAQPage items={faq} />
	<script>
		import { mountStarBattle } from '../../scripts/starbattle-ui.ts';
		const root = document.getElementById('game');
		if (root) mountStarBattle(root);
	</script>
</Layout>
